  "Question": "The expressions \\(a+bc\\) and \\((a+b)(a+c)\\) are:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "always equal",
    "B": "never equal",
    "C": "equal when \\(a+b+c=1\\)",
    "D": "equal when \\(a+b+c=0\\)",
    "E": "equal only when \\(a=b=c=0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Subtracting the expressions reveals exactly when they are equal. Expanding and collecting terms gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a+b)(a+c)-(a+bc)\n&=a^2+ab+ac+bc-a-bc\\\\\n&=a(a+b+c-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis product is zero precisely when \\(a=0\\) or \\(a+b+c=1\\). We have not divided by \\(a\\), so the case \\(a=0\\) is retained.\n\nIn particular, whenever \\(a+b+c=1\\), the expressions are equal. The word “when” states a sufficient condition, so it allows equality in other cases too. The correct choice is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
